From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J: start the JVM with -Xrs, leaving process signals to the player

libbluray creates the JVM inside the player's process. Without -Xrs HotSpot
installs its own SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP/SIGQUIT handlers, so once a BD-J disc
has been played, a SIGTERM to the player (systemctl stop kodi) is taken by the
JVM: it runs its shutdown sequence and calls exit() on its own thread. C++
static destructors then run under Kodi's live threads - GUIFontManager freeing
fonts the renderer is drawing with (FT_Done_Face SIGSEGV), the joystick
peripheral add-on unloaded under its event thread ("pure virtual method
called") - and Kodi dies with a core instead of shutting down.

Measured on the AM9 Pro (2026-10-02): stop after a BD-J disc 2/2 crashed,
stop without BD-J 2/2 clean, stop after BD-J with -Xrs (JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS)
3/3 clean.

--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
@@ -943,6 +943,11 @@
     option[n++].optionString = str_dup   ("-Xms256M");
     option[n++].optionString = str_dup   ("-Xmx256M");
     option[n++].optionString = str_dup   ("-Xss2048k");
+    /* The JVM runs inside the player's process: leave SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP/SIGQUIT to
+     * the player. Without -Xrs HotSpot installs its own handlers, and a SIGTERM (a
+     * service stop) makes it call exit() on its own thread - static destructors then
+     * run under the player's live threads and it crashes on the way down. */
+    option[n++].optionString = str_dup   ("-Xrs");
     option[n++].optionString = str_dup   ("-Djava.io.tmpdir=/storage/.kodi/userdata/addon_data/tools.jre.zulu");
     BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "Use /storage/.kodi/userdata/addon_data/tools.jre.zulu as cache dir.\n");
     option[n++].optionString = str_dup   ("-Djava.awt.headless=false");
